-- sql/field_scope_live_fixtures_2026-10-28.sql
--
-- CONCEPT. This file provisions the audit licences, credentials and fixture rows
-- that `python tools/sairn_field_scope_check.py --live` needs to read four rules
-- off the DEPLOYED endpoint: sen_caregivers, sen_settings, rf_settings and
-- alf_facility.
--
-- WRITTEN AND RUN BY cc b63 ON MICHAEL'S INSTRUCTION (items 1-2: live blocks for
-- the three rules that had none, plus audit-role credentials for them). It
-- follows the shape of sql/sen_audit_0002_role_scope_credentials_2026-10-28.sql:
--   - a licence row, `on conflict do nothing`;
--   - hashPin() credentials;
--   - a PR 3.4 recoverability guard;
--   - a salt check on every credential;
--   - a fixture-count guard on every table.
--
-- WHY NEW LICENCES AND NOT RF-AUDIT-2026 / ALF-AUDIT-2026.
--   - The judge is closed-world, so every row a role can read must be KNOWN.
--   - The existing audit licences hold other probes' rows.
--   - Upserting a marker-valued damage_threshold onto RF-AUDIT-2026 would
--     overwrite the value the roofing probes run against.
--   - The digits are a SERIAL, not a year. The key shape is
--     ^[A-Z]{2,6}-AUDIT-\d{4}$, and each app's client accepts only its own
--     prefix (sairnroofing.html takes RF-, sairncare.html takes ALF-).
--
-- WHERE THE FIXTURE VALUES COME FROM. They are generated by importing
-- tools/sairn_field_scope_check.py's own seed() over tools/field_scope_rules.json.
-- Each null in a rule becomes the same deterministic marker string (FSMK...Q)
-- that the live judge searches the response for. A {"marker": ...} cell becomes
-- the literal it names; updated_at is timestamptz and cannot hold FSMK..., so
-- its marker is a distinctive 2001-02-03 instant. Nothing in this file was
-- re-typed by hand. Change a rule and this file must be regenerated, or the
-- live judge will report MISSING, which is loud.
--
-- sen_caregivers on SEN-AUDIT-0002: b62's two plain rows (CG-FS-SELF and
-- CG-FS-PEER) are UPDATED into the marker-valued fixtures, so the licence still
-- holds exactly two rows. fs-caregiver owns the self row; fs-peer, which has no
-- credential, owns the peer row.
--
-- ── THE ACCOUNTS. THE PINS ARE PUBLISHED ON PURPOSE ────────────────────────
-- The reason, and the condition, are the same as in the 2026-10-20 and b62
-- files: audit licences hold no customer data. Rotate every PIN the day one of
-- them does.
--   licence         employee           role            PIN
--   RF-AUDIT-0002   fs-owner           owner           PIN 28528653
--   RF-AUDIT-0002   fs-admin           admin           PIN 65138684
--   RF-AUDIT-0002   fs-estimator       estimator       PIN 61452245
--   RF-AUDIT-0002   fs-foreman         foreman         PIN 32313368
--   RF-AUDIT-0002   fs-crew            crew            PIN 20734995
--   ALF-AUDIT-0002  fs-owner           owner           PIN 86576619
--   ALF-AUDIT-0002  fs-billing         billing         PIN 78310947
--   ALF-AUDIT-0002  fs-nursing         nursing         PIN 57096965
--   ALF-AUDIT-0002  fs-med-aide        med_aide        PIN 33024433
--   ALF-AUDIT-0002  fs-caregiver       caregiver       PIN 86286640
--   ALF-AUDIT-0002  fs-activities      activities      PIN 47801717
--   ALF-AUDIT-0002  fs-office-manager  office_manager  PIN 17478561
--   SEN-AUDIT-0002's five accounts are unchanged; see the b62 file.
--
-- HOW THE HASHES WERE MADE.
--   - Each PIN went through api/_lib/auth.js hashPin().
--   - Each hash was passed back through its own verifyPin(). The PIN returned
--     true and 00000000 returned false, for all twelve.
--   - PROVISIONING_ROLES, read from api/rf-auth.js:139 and api/alf-auth.js:52:
--     ['owner'].
--
-- IF RUN TWICE: no new rows are created, and the same values are written again.
-- The b62 rename UPDATE matches nothing the second time; the upserts that follow
-- it hold the rows. Any failing guard rolls back the whole transaction.
-- ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

begin;

